# AMD64 thunk export spec -- oleaut32.dll.
#
# Greedy-cover module from the 2026-08-14 triage worklist: with combase,
# ole32, advapi32, oleaut32 and gdi32 present as guest thunk builtins, 101
# more of the 344 corpus tests become loadable.  Exports the oracle refuses
# (struct-by-value, float, no declaration) are absent and bind to a named
# per-symbol sentinel -- loadability first, surface closes from the data.
DLL oleaut32.dll
FROM-SPEC auto

# --- system-COM interface roster + the flat-surface interception ---
# (hangover-ppc64le/docs/system-com-design.md).  COM-JSON adds the
# per-interface vtable stub arrays and __wine_com_thunk_info; the guest
# gets proxies whose vtables are AMD64 trap stubs, dispatched by native
# combase's __wine_com_dispatch.  Every flat export whose signature
# carries an interface pointer is classified below; the build-time audit
# (spec2thunk) FAILS if any is left to pass through silently.
COM-JSON  ../../ppc64le/syscom/interfaces_syscom.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 2026-08-17 DOOM triage: the six ordinals MICROSOFT'S mfc140u.dll imports
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DOOM (2016) loads Razer's CChromaEditorLibrary.dll, which pulls in
# Microsoft's real mfc140u.dll.  mfc140u imports OLEAUT32 BY ORDINAL and
# carries no names at all -- 113 and 417..421 -- and the guest loader reported
# each as "No implementation for OLEAUT32.dll.<n>" and bound a sentinel.
#
# The ordinals themselves were never wrong: assign_ordinals() already pins each
# export at the number oleaut32.spec gives it, so 417 IS OleCreatePropertyFrame
# here exactly as it is in Microsoft's module.  They were HOLES because the
# exports behind them were oracle-refused, and a hole is what the loader read.
# Five of the six are the OLE control surface, declared in include/olectl.h,
# which nothing already in the oracle's translation unit pulls in -- the same
# tooling gap, not an ABI fact.  One PROBE-EXTRA closes all five.
PROBE-EXTRA   olectl.h        # ordinals 417..421 + the rest of the Ole* control surface

# Ordinal 113 (VarBstrFromCy) STAYS A HOLE.  It takes CY by value; CY is a
# union of two 32-bit halves and a LONGLONG, so it is an 8-byte aggregate, and
# this generator classifies aggregates as unrepresentable rather than modelling
# the ABI rule that would in fact pass it in one register.  Right by
# coincidence is not right.  Nothing on mfc140u's load path calls it (measured
# -- the module initialises and its exports run); a guest that formats a
# currency through it gets the named sentinel instead of a wrong answer.

# audited pass-throughs: the interface-bearing type is inert here
GUEST-PASS   VariantInit                      writes VT_EMPTY and reads no interface pointer

# hand walker (system-com-design.md §9.2): VARIANT is a by-value carrier that
# CAN hold VT_UNKNOWN/VT_DISPATCH, so the export classifies the payload
# instead of passing it straight to native -- a scalar/BSTR/BYREF/bad-vt
# VARIANT still reaches native untouched, but a proxy inside VT_UNKNOWN/
# VT_DISPATCH has its guest-visible reference dropped through the proxy, and
# a guest-implemented object or an interface-element SAFEARRAY/VT_RECORD is
# refused by name.  See dlls/combase/syscom.c __wine_guest_VariantClear.
GUEST-IMPL   VariantClear                     __wine_guest_VariantClear

# refused loudly (E_NOTIMPL) until their wrapper/walker lands -- never
# a pass-through that would hand the guest a native vtable
GUEST-REFUSE BstrFromVector
GUEST-REFUSE CreateDispTypeInfo
GUEST-REFUSE CreateErrorInfo
GUEST-REFUSE CreateStdDispatch
GUEST-REFUSE DispCallFunc
GUEST-REFUSE DispGetIDsOfNames
GUEST-REFUSE DispGetParam
GUEST-REFUSE DispInvoke
GUEST-REFUSE DllGetClassObject
GUEST-REFUSE GetActiveObject
GUEST-REFUSE GetErrorInfo
GUEST-REFUSE GetRecordInfoFromGuids
GUEST-REFUSE GetRecordInfoFromTypeInfo
GUEST-REFUSE LPSAFEARRAY_UserFree
GUEST-REFUSE LPSAFEARRAY_UserMarshal
GUEST-REFUSE LPSAFEARRAY_UserSize
GUEST-REFUSE LPSAFEARRAY_UserUnmarshal
GUEST-REFUSE LoadRegTypeLib
GUEST-REFUSE LoadTypeLib
GUEST-REFUSE LoadTypeLibEx

# The OLE control surface reached via ordinals 417..421 (see the PROBE-EXTRA
# note above).  Every one of these hands back a native interface pointer --
# IPicture, IFont, IUnknown -- through an out slot, so a pass-through would put
# host ppc64 vtables in guest-visible memory and the guest's first method call
# would execute them as x86-64.  Refused loudly, which is what the ordinal
# needed: the ORDINAL is now filled and the import binds, instead of the guest
# reading a hole and getting a sentinel it cannot name.
GUEST-REFUSE OleCreateFontIndirect
GUEST-REFUSE OleCreatePictureIndirect
GUEST-REFUSE OleCreatePropertyFrame
GUEST-REFUSE OleLoadPicture
GUEST-REFUSE OleLoadPictureEx
GUEST-REFUSE OleLoadPicturePath
GUEST-REFUSE OleSavePictureFile
GUEST-REFUSE RegisterActiveObject
GUEST-REFUSE RegisterTypeLib
GUEST-REFUSE RegisterTypeLibForUser
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayAccessData
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayAllocData
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayAllocDescriptor
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayAllocDescriptorEx
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayCopy
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayCopyData
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayCreate
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayCreateEx
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayCreateVector
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayCreateVectorEx
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayDestroy
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayDestroyData
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayDestroyDescriptor
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayGetDim
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayGetElement
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayGetElemsize
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayGetIID
GUEST-REFUSE SafeArrayGetLBound
